<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Fireworks!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Create a title or caption for a paragraph or image.
Reading: Show comprehension of simple texts.
Phonemic Awareness: Recognize all the letters of the alphabet in isolation and the correspondence between letter and sound at the beginning of words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can create a title or caption for a drawing or image related to festival foods or activities (e.g., writing "Delicious Food at the Festival" for a picture of festival foods).
Can show comprehension of short, simple sentences about festivals and celebrations (e.g., "Look at the fireworks.").
Can recognize the initial letter and its corresponding sound in festival-related words (e.g., identifying the letter "P" and its /p/ sound in "party").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of festival items (fireworks, cotton candy, music, dance). The teacher holds up a "fireworks" flashcard and says, "Look! Fireworks! They are bright!" Students repeat. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and encourages one-word answers or pointing. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large picture of a festival with fireworks. The teacher points to a simple sentence written below it: "I like the fireworks." The teacher slowly reads the sentence, pointing to each word as it is read. The teacher emphasizes the /f/ sound in "fireworks." (3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher shows the sentence "I like the fireworks." again.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: (Pointing to "fireworks") "Is this a festival cake or festival lights?" (Expected answer: "Lights!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: (Pointing to "I like") "Do I love them or not like them?" (Expected answer: "Love them!" or "Like them!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large-format, illustrated short descriptive paragraph titled "A Fun Festival Day." The text should be simple sentences such as: "It's festival season! I like the fireworks. They are bright. I like cotton candy. It is sweet. The music is loud! It is fun!" The text is presented on a whiteboard or projector, with corresponding pictures for each sentence. (7 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Show comprehension of simple texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the entire text aloud, pointing to the words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the text about? (Festival or school?)" Students point or say. "What do I like in the text?" (Fireworks or homework?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads sentences and students show thumbs up (True) or thumbs down (False) based on the text. Example: "The fireworks are quiet." (False). "Cotton candy is sweet." (True).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple descriptive sentences (Subject + verb + adjective: "It is fun!", "They are bright!") and present simple ("I like the meal."). Vocabulary: festival, fireworks, cotton candy, fun, bright, loud, music. Phonemic awareness: P /p/ "party", F /f/ "fireworks", M /m/ "meal".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Read and Match:</b> Students receive a worksheet with festival pictures (e.g., fireworks, cotton candy, music, party) and simple sentences or words to match. Example: Picture of fireworks -> read "fireworks" or "They are bright." Picture of cotton candy -> read "cotton candy" or "It is sweet." Students draw a line from the sentence to the correct picture. (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Fill in the Blank (Word Bank):</b> Students read simple sentences with a missing word related to the festival theme. They choose the correct word from a provided word bank (e.g., "fireworks", "fun", "music"). Example: "I like the _____. (fireworks/music)" or "It is _____. (fun/sad)". This activity also reinforces initial sound recognition for words like "fun" (/f/) or "music" (/m/). (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Read and Circle Initial Sound:</b> The teacher provides a list of festival-related words from the text (e.g., **F**ireworks, **M**usic, **P**arty, **F**un). Students read each word and then circle the first letter. The teacher guides them to pronounce the initial sound /f/, /m/, /p/ as they circle. For example, "Circle the 'F' in Fireworks." (5 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Create a Caption.</b> Students will receive a worksheet with three different pictures of festival scenes or items (e.g., a picture of bright fireworks, a picture of kids eating cotton candy, a picture of people dancing to music). Below each picture, there will be two simple pre-written captions. Students will read both captions and circle the one that best describes the picture. For higher-level students, they might write one word from a word bank as a caption. (8 min)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example for picture of fireworks:
                        <br> A) "I like the fireworks. They are bright."
                        <br> B) "I like cotton candy. It is sweet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to read the captions aloud quietly before choosing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension to select the most appropriate caption for a given image, demonstrating understanding of simple descriptive sentences related to the festival theme.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the caption creation activity. The teacher circulates, observing students' ability to read the captions and choose the correct one, and their recognition of initial sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete the "Create a Caption" worksheet from Stage 4 individually. The teacher collects the worksheets.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can select the correct caption for at least 2 out of 3 images.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows comprehension of simple descriptive sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to recognize initial letters and sounds in words (e.g., 'F' for fireworks).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper slip with two prompts:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about _____" (Students draw a picture of something they can read about, like fireworks, or write one word).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reading about festivals is: Easy / Okay / Hard" (Students circle one).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was the easiest part to read today about festivals?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new word did you learn to read today?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading strategies worked well for the students (e.g., matching pictures to text, circling initial sounds)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the texts and captions at the appropriate CEFR Pre-A1.4 level? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled with decoding or comprehension?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I further encourage phonemic awareness during reading activities?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the vocabulary and reading comprehension practiced today to actively speak and interact in pairs, describing their favorite festival parts and foods.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Date] <br> [Time]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>